WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:04.640
Pixar has spent years making films that are
enjoyable to kids and adults alike.

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:09.140
From Toy Story to Coco, the studio has become
a favorite of moviegoers of all ages.

00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:13.620
Onward is no exception to the rule, building
a magical kid-friendly adventure in between

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:15.960
some pretty adult topics and jokes.

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:20.480
Onward follows Ian and Barley Lightfoot as
they try to bring back their dad from the

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:22.800
dead for a single day in order to get to know
him.

00:00:23.089 --> 00:00:27.060
Unfortunately, Ian only brings back the bottom
half of their dad, leading to a quest to find

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:31.320
a magic gem that can bring back his upper
half before he completely fades away at sundown.

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:35.860
As the two and a half men go about their urgent
quest, there's plenty of Weekend at Bernie's-esque

00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:38.800
shenanigans and magical mixups to delight
the kids.

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:43.020
However, adults might find themselves wondering
why the movie's main story is about two kids

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:44.780
bonding with their dad's butt.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:47.640
As Uproxx's Mike Ryan so eloquently summarized:

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:51.520
"This is a movie about two kids resurrecting
their dead dad's crotch and it's just the

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:52.520
WEIRDEST thing."

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:57.140
While Pixar has managed to stay beloved over
the years, other animation studios have stepped

00:00:57.140 --> 00:00:57.860
up their game.

00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:02.320
Disney's non-Pixar animation studio brought
the world Frozen and Tangled, while DreamWorks

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:05.540
had a massive franchise hit on its hands with
How to Train Your Dragon.

00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:09.960
Still, even as other studios have begun to
match Pixar's artistry, Pixar movies feel

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:14.360
unique, with an incomparable sense of style
and personality that shines through.

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:16.580
Until Onward, that is.

00:01:16.810 --> 00:01:21.680
The 2020 movie feels like Pixar slapped a
logo over a DreamWorks film, from the character

00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:24.110
designs to the dancing sequence in the middle.

00:01:24.110 --> 00:01:28.780
In fact, "Onward looks like a DreamWorks movie"
was a common refrain when the trailer first

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:29.280
dropped.

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:33.100
While it might not go so far as to have a
dance sequence as the credits roll, that might

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:36.880
not stop some adults from briefly forgetting
which studio made Onward.

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:41.760
About halfway through Onward, Ian and Barley
Lightfoot get pulled over by some fantasy

00:01:41.880 --> 00:01:42.380
cops.

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:47.020
One, a cyclops played by Lena Waithe, offhandedly
mentions her "girlfriend's daughter."

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:51.340
It's a small moment for a character that really
only shows up in a very small scene.

00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:55.080
Still, adults will likely catch the implication
that Waithe's character is gay.

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:59.000
However, considering the brief screen time
for the character, and the even briefer mention

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:02.600
of the character's girlfriend, it's unlikely
that children will catch the meaning.

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:07.460
It's wonderful that Disney is acknowledging
the existence of LGBTQ+ people in the world

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:11.620
of their films, but like the brief lesbian
kiss in Star Wars: The Rise of Skywalker,

00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:13.790
these moments tend to be fairly insubstantial.

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:18.160
Hopefully, the existence of people along a
gender and sexual spectrum won't be something

00:02:18.170 --> 00:02:20.450
that only adults notice in films going forward.

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:25.310
In one notable set piece, Ian and Barley have
to figure out a way to cross a bottomless

00:02:25.310 --> 00:02:27.320
pit in order to continue their quest.

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:32.300
Barley tells Ian about a spell that will let
him walk on air by creating an invisible bridge

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:35.120
that continues to exist as long as he truly
believes in it.

00:02:35.790 --> 00:02:40.040
The safety rope falls away while Ian's halfway
across, but he's already got the confidence

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:42.340
to cross the bridge by himself and doesn't
notice.

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:45.980
Children might be on the edge of their seats
hoping that Ian makes it across, but the whole

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:49.080
scene will likely feel very familiar to the
adults watching.

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:54.080
The whole set piece feels a bit like team-building
exercises like the "trust fall" or walking

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:58.860
on hot coals - or the invisible bridge Indiana
Jones had to walk across in The Last Crusade.

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:03.300
For the majority of the runtime of Onward,
viewers are likely empathizing with Ian's

00:03:03.310 --> 00:03:04.930
quest to get to know his dad.

00:03:04.930 --> 00:03:08.510
He's the character who seems most outwardly
affected by his father's death, and he's the

00:03:08.510 --> 00:03:11.180
one who seems most eager to bring him back
as soon as possible.

00:03:11.670 --> 00:03:15.220
Near the end of the film, however, Barley
reveals that he has deep regrets over not

00:03:15.230 --> 00:03:18.470
getting the chance to say goodbye to his dad
before he passed away.

00:03:18.470 --> 00:03:22.340
During the scene, the adults in the audience
might realize that Barley's voice actor, Chris

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:26.840
Pratt, has some practice playing an aging
man-child struggling to get over the traumatic

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:29.060
death of a parent due to a sudden sickness.

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:33.819
After all, that describes Barley just as well
as it describes Peter Quill in the Guardians

00:03:33.820 --> 00:03:34.760
of the Galaxy movies.

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:35.860
"Star lord."

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:38.360
"Who?"

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:41.580
"Star Lord, man, legendary outlaw?"

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:46.980
Even though Onward is ostensibly set in the
"modern" world with touchscreen cell phones,

00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:49.840
there's a distinctly '80s sensibility to the
environment.

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:54.420
Barley drives a van with a fantasy decal on
the side, plays a game similar to Dungeons

00:03:54.430 --> 00:03:58.659
&amp; Dragons, and both Ian and Barley listen
to cassette decks instead of streaming music

00:03:58.660 --> 00:03:59.900
or even CDs.

00:04:00.100 --> 00:04:03.540
Even their mother Laurel gets her exercise
by watching a workout tape.

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:06.250
As The Atlantic's David Sims wrote in his
review:

00:04:06.380 --> 00:04:10.819
"Onward is informed by the '80s aesthetic
of tabletop games, minivans airbrushed with

00:04:10.820 --> 00:04:13.920
heavy-metal album-cover art, and cookie-cutter
suburban life..."

00:04:14.370 --> 00:04:18.160
While plenty of kids wouldn't even recognize
a cassette, nostalgic adults might recall

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:22.729
their own days playing Dungeons &amp; Dragons
and listening to '80s power ballads - or at

00:04:22.729 --> 00:04:24.900
least watching films that reference those
moments.

00:04:25.660 --> 00:04:29.260
Plot holes don't tend to matter much in a
world that literally has magic in it.

00:04:29.500 --> 00:04:33.330
Still, there are some rules to the magic in
Onward, and one moment near the end of the

00:04:33.330 --> 00:04:35.720
film may leave adults scratching their heads.

00:04:36.050 --> 00:04:40.280
During the battle with the dragon, Ian loses
his dad's magical staff, but manages to make

00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:44.120
his own by enlarging a splinter that had gotten
stuck in his hand earlier in the film.

00:04:44.410 --> 00:04:48.320
In the epilogue, Ian can be seen giving a
talk to a group of students as he stands with

00:04:48.330 --> 00:04:49.940
his magical splinter staff.

00:04:50.110 --> 00:04:54.260
However, earlier in the film, it's established
that Barley's accidental shrinking because

00:04:54.260 --> 00:04:56.300
of the spell misfiring is temporary.

00:04:56.620 --> 00:05:00.820
While kids are enjoying the epilogue, adults
might be wondering whether objects made larger

00:05:00.820 --> 00:05:04.560
are also temporary, or whether that only applies
to shrunk objects.

00:05:04.840 --> 00:05:08.780
If Ian can make things permanently larger,
does this lead to a Cloudy With a Chance of

00:05:08.780 --> 00:05:10.000
Meatballs-type situation?

00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:13.560
Or does Ian just grow his splinter whenever
he wants to use his staff?

00:05:13.860 --> 00:05:15.100
Can anyone explain this?

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:16.620
"Please to explain this?"

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:21.280
"Well, whenever you notice something like
that, a wizard did it."

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:23.980
"I see, alright, yes, but in episode AG4-"

00:05:23.980 --> 00:05:24.540
"Wizard."

